/* data/learn-c/chem-12-07.js - GENERATED by tools/build_learn.py from data/learn/summaries/chem-12-07.json (our own words;
 * the notes of one chapter, loaded when the chapter is opened). Do not edit by hand. */
(window.LEARN_C = window.LEARN_C || {})["chem-12-07"] = {"7.1":{"s":"In p-block elements the last electron enters a p orbital of the outer shell. This chapter covers the last three p-block groups. They are group 16 (oxygen family), group 17 (halogens) and group 18 (noble gases). It covers their trends and important compounds."},"7.2":{"s":"Group 16 is O, S, Se, Te and Po, the chalcogens ('ore formers'), because many ores are oxides or sulfides. Group 17 is F, Cl, Br, I and At, the halogens ('salt formers'), found mostly as halide salts. Group 18 is He, Ne, Ar, Kr, Xe and Rn, present in small amounts in air; radon is radioactive."},"7.3":{"s":"Outer electron configurations: group 16 is ns²np⁴, group 17 is ns²np⁵, and group 18 is ns²np⁶, a full octet (helium is 1s²). These configurations explain the valencies: group 16 needs two electrons, group 17 needs one, and group 18 needs none."},"7.4":{"s":"Down each group, atomic and ionic size grow and ionisation enthalpy falls. Electronegativity is highest at the top (fluorine is the most electronegative element). Metallic character rises down group 16 (polonium is a metal). Melting and boiling points rise down the groups as van der Waals forces grow. Halogens are coloured.","w":["Electron gain enthalpy is most negative for chlorine, not fluorine: fluorine's small size crowds the added electron."]},"7.5":{"s":"The first member of a group behaves differently because it is small, very electronegative and has no d orbitals. Oxygen is diatomic (O₂) while sulfur is S₈. Oxygen forms hydrogen bonds, so water is a liquid while H₂S is a gas. Oxygen's covalency is limited to 4. Fluorine shows similar unusual behaviour in group 17.","w":["Fluorine shows only −1 and forms only one oxoacid (HOF), because it has no d orbitals and is the most electronegative element."]},"7.6":{"s":"Common oxidation states are −2 for group 16 and −1 for group 17. The heavier members also show +2, +4 and +6 (group 16) or +1, +3, +5 and +7 (group 17), using their d orbitals. Fluorine shows only −1. The chapter compares their hydrides, oxides and halides, and their reactivity, which falls down group 17.","w":["Acidic strength of hydrides rises down the group (H₂O < H₂S < H₂Se < H₂Te; HF < HCl < HBr < HI) as the H–X bond weakens."]},"7.7":{"s":"Allotropy means one element existing in different forms. Oxygen exists as O₂ and ozone, O₃. Sulfur exists as rhombic (α) sulfur, stable below about 369 K, and monoclinic (β) sulfur above it; both are built from S₈ rings. Selenium, tellurium and polonium also have allotropes."},"7.8":{"s":"Oxoacids contain an element, oxygen and acidic hydrogen. Sulfur forms many, the best known being sulfuric acid H₂SO₄ and sulfurous acid H₂SO₃. Halogens form hypohalous, halous, halic and perhalic acids (for chlorine, HOCl to HClO₄). Acid strength rises as the oxidation state of the central atom rises.","w":["For chlorine oxoacids acid strength rises HOCl < HClO₂ < HClO₃ < HClO₄, but oxidising power falls in the same order."]},"7.9":{"s":"Oxygen (dioxygen) is prepared, for example, by heating KClO₃ with MnO₂. It forms oxides that are acidic (SO₂), basic (Na₂O), amphoteric (Al₂O₃) or neutral (CO). Ozone, O₃, is a strong oxidising agent and forms a protective layer in the upper atmosphere."},"7.10":{"s":"Sulfur dioxide is made by burning sulfur. It is an acidic gas and a reducing (bleaching) agent. Sulfuric acid is made by the contact process. SO₂ is oxidised to SO₃ over V₂O₅. The SO₃ is absorbed in sulfuric acid to form oleum, which is then diluted. The acid is a dehydrating and oxidising agent.","w":["SO₂ bleaches by reduction (temporary); Cl₂ bleaches by oxidation (permanent)."]},"7.11":{"s":"Chlorine is a greenish-yellow gas, made by oxidising HCl, for example with MnO₂. It is a strong oxidising and bleaching agent; the bleaching works through oxidation in moist conditions. Hydrogen chloride is a colourless gas that dissolves in water to give hydrochloric acid."},"7.12":{"s":"Halogens combine with each other to form interhalogen compounds. Their types are XX′, XX′₃, XX′₅ and XX′₇, where X is the larger halogen (ClF, BrF₃, IF₅, IF₇). They are more reactive than the halogens themselves, except fluorine, because the X–X′ bond is weaker.","w":["Interhalogens have an even total number of halogen atoms (XX′, XX′₃, XX′₅, XX′₇) and are diamagnetic."]},"7.13":{"s":"Noble gases were thought to be inert, but xenon has a low enough ionisation enthalpy to react with fluorine. It forms XeF₂, XeF₄ and XeF₆, and oxygen compounds such as XeO₃. Their shapes follow VSEPR theory: XeF₂ is linear and XeF₄ is square planar.","w":["XeF₂ linear, XeF₄ square planar, XeF₆ distorted octahedral, XeO₃ pyramidal (VSEPR with lone pairs on Xe)."]}};
